:false,"_score_sbc_dilution":37.4,"_gate_insider_own":0.02128,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":14.2,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Omnicell, Inc., together with its subsidiaries, provides healthcare technology in the United States and internationally. It offers hospital and health systems solutions, such as points of care for clinician workflows in patient care areas of the healthcare system; Titan XT, an automated dispensing system; XTExtend, a console swap for its XT cabinets; and Central Pharmacy Dispensing Service for the medication dispensing process. The company also provides Central Med Automation Service for medication dispensing; IV Compounding Service, an in-house compounding system; specialty pharmacy services, including turnkey solution to help health systems establish, manage, and optimize an entity-owned specialty pharmacy; EnlivenHealth platform to digitally enable retail and community pharmacies; medication adherence solutions comprising consumables and medication packaging systems; and technology implementation, customer education and training, program management, and related offerings to professional services. In addition, it offers post-installation support and maintenance via phone and/or web, on-site service, parts, and access to software upgrades; software and hardware products for full traceability of medicines and medical supplies throughout the healthcare system; OmniSphere, a cloud-based platform. The company was formerly known as Omnicell Technologies, Inc. and changed its name to Omnicell, Inc. in 2001. Omnicell, Inc. was incorporated in 1992 and is headquartered in Fort Worth, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Randall A. Lipps | Founder, Executive Chairman & CEO | Age 68 | Compensation: $2.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 3K people. Revenue per employee of $343K ranks in the below the sector median among Healthcare peers, with revenue growing 12%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $25K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $13K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 7\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Omnicell, Inc. is a small-cap healthcare business with a $1.6B market cap on $1.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -3% over the past three years and +9% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 0% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 47%.","The business throws off $87M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 7 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 62.04\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin 2.3\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 4.8% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 2.1% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Priced near or above estimated fair value, so the margin of safety has been bid away","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.03568238193076041},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 9.9% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Healthcare peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Significant goodwill (37% of assets) from past acquisitions \u2014 a soft asset a conservative appraisal discounts to zero","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Patent cliffs, drug-pricing reform, and reimbursement pressure \u2014 a single regulatory change can reset the pricing power that justifies the multiple.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"GLP-1 disruption across sub-sectors \u2014 weight-loss drugs are reshaping demand for orthopaedics, cardiovascular devices, and sleep-apnea treatments in ways not yet priced in.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Founder-led \u2014 owner alignment that often matters more than any incentive plan","Financial quality scores above most Healthcare peers","Healthcare sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 13% over 3 months","Healthcare ETF in a sustained uptrend above its long-term average","Strong 6-month sector performance (30.7% return)"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Omnicell (NASDAQ:OMCL) Share Price Breaks Below 50-Day Moving Average \u2013 Should You Sell?","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/13/omnicell-nasdaqomcl-share-price-breaks-below-50-day-moving-average-should-you-sell.html","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794568927.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.84,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"There 